<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Taifa of Arcos</h1><p class="st-meta">1011 CE – 1068 CE (approx.) · capital Arcos de la Frontera</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Berber-ruled taifa centred on the clifftop town of Arcos de la Frontera, one of several absorbed by the expanding Abbadids of Seville.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>During the fitna after 1009, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> troops who had served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphs</a> settled in the hill towns of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. Around 1011 the Banu Khizrun, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanata-berbers">Zanata Berber</a> family, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcos-de-la-frontera">Arcos</a> their seat and ruled it as an independent principality.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcos-de-la-frontera">Arcos</a> was a fortified town controlling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalete">Guadalete</a> valley and routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerez-de-la-frontera">Jerez</a> and the Strait. Like the other small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifas</a> of the region, it relied on its walls and on alliances with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammudid-dynasty">Hammudids</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/taifa-of-ronda">Taifa of Ronda</a> against Seville. Only the outline of its rulers' succession is known.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbadid-dynasty">Abbadid</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutadid-of-seville">al-Mutadid</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/taifa-of-seville">Taifa of Seville</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcos-de-la-frontera">Arcos</a> in the 1050s or 1060s as he swept up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> principalities of the south-west. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> took the town in the 13th century.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/taifa-of-arcos.jpg" alt="Taifa of Arcos" width="320" height="271"><figcaption>Image: Tyk · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Location_map_Taifa_of_Arcos.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1011 CE – 1068 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Arcos de la Frontera</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Muhammad ibn Khizrun</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Andalusi Arabic, Berber</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Arcos</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1040&amp;focus=taifa-of-arcos" aria-label="Taifa of Arcos on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4534 853 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Taifa of Arcos lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4854 1109L4881 1071M4854 1109L4850 1088"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4881 1071h0M4850 1088h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4854 1109h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4854 1109h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Arcos de la Frontera. <a href="/atlas?year=1040&amp;focus=taifa-of-arcos">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
